.Dd September 25, 2026
.Dt SETITIMER 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm setitimer ,
.Nm getitimer ,
.Nm alarm
.Nd set and read the real-time interval timer
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/time.h
.Ft int
.Fn setitimer "int which" "const struct itimerval *new" "struct itimerval *old"
.Ft int
.Fn getitimer "int which" "struct itimerval *cur"
.In unistd.h
.Ft unsigned
.Fn alarm "unsigned seconds"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
Each process has one interval timer,
.Dv ITIMER_REAL ,
which counts real time and posts
.Dv SIGALRM
to the process when it expires.
.Pp
.Fn setitimer
arms it:
.Va it_value
is the time to the first
.Dv SIGALRM
and
.Va it_interval
the time between each later one, or zero for a single one.
A zero
.Va it_value
disarms the timer.
Times are rounded up to whole 10 ms ticks, and the tick in progress is
not counted, so the first
.Dv SIGALRM
never comes before the time asked for, up to the limit below.
At the tick that posts a
.Dv SIGALRM ,
the timer is armed again for the interval.
Unless
.Fa old
is null, it receives the timer's setting before the call, as
.Fn getitimer
reports it.
.Pp
The timer tick posts the signal, which wakes the process if it is asleep
in a call;
.Xr nanosleep 2 ,
.Xr poll 2
and
.Xr select 2
say when that ends their call with
.Er EINTR ,
and
.Xr sigaction 2
says it for other waits.
This kernel does not restart an interrupted call, whatever the handler's
.Dv SA_RESTART
says
.Pq Xr sigaction 2 .
.Pp
.Fn getitimer
reports the time left before the next
.Dv SIGALRM ,
zero when the timer is disarmed, and the interval.
The time left is the whole ticks to the due tick less the tick in
progress, and one tick at least while the timer is armed, since zero
means disarmed.
So a value read in the timer's last tick and passed straight back to
.Fn setitimer
moves the timer a tick later.
.Pp
.Fn alarm
arms a single
.Dv SIGALRM
.Fa seconds
from now, or disarms the timer for 0.
It returns the seconds that were left before the alarm it replaces, zero
when none was armed.
musl's
.Fn alarm
calls
.Fn setitimer
and rounds any fraction of a second up; the raw call rounds to the
nearest second, and returns one when less than a second was left.
.Pp
A child of
.Xr fork 2
starts with the timer disarmed.
.Xr execve 2
keeps it, but clears the pending signals, so a
.Dv SIGALRM
posted and not yet delivered is lost.
.Ss Differences from Linux
.Dv ITIMER_VIRTUAL
and
.Dv ITIMER_PROF
are refused with
.Er EINVAL :
they count a process's own processor time, which this kernel does not
keep.
The timer counts 10 ms ticks, so the signal is posted at a tick, and
.Fn getitimer
reports whole ticks where Linux reports microseconds.
The raw call refuses a null
.Fa new
with
.Er EFAULT ,
where Linux disarms the timer, and the kernel checks
.Fa which
before it reads
.Fa new .
Linux arms a periodic timer again when its
.Dv SIGALRM
is delivered, so while the signal is blocked its timer stays expired and
.Fn getitimer
reports an
.Va it_value
of zero with the interval; this kernel arms it again at the tick that
posts the signal, whatever the mask, and reports the time to the next.
Linux keeps the pending signals across
.Xr execve 2 .
.Ss System call interface
The i386 system call numbers are 104 for
.Fn setitimer ,
105 for
.Fn getitimer
and 27 for
.Fn alarm .
EBX holds
.Fa which ,
or the seconds for
.Fn alarm ;
ECX points to the new or current value and EDX to the old one.
The kernel's itimerval is four signed 32-bit fields: the interval's
seconds and microseconds, then the value's.
The kernel limits the seconds it counts to 21474836, as
.Xr nanosleep 2
does, and a time to 2147483645 ticks, about 248.55 days; a longer time
is shortened to that.
.Pp
musl passes that form for its 64-bit
.Vt time_t ,
and refuses with
.Er ENOTSUP ,
before the call, seconds that do not fit in 32 bits, so its
.Fn alarm
of more than 2147483647 seconds arms nothing, leaves an alarm already
armed as it was, and returns 0.
Its
.Fn setitimer
reads
.Fa new
itself before the call, so a null or unreadable
.Fa new
faults in the caller, whatever
.Fa which
is.
It writes
.Fa old ,
when that is not null, only after the call succeeds, and its
.Fn getitimer
writes
.Fa cur
only after the call succeeds, so an unwritable
.Fa old
or
.Fa cur
faults in the caller with the call done.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn setitimer
and
.Fn getitimer
return 0 on success, or -1 on failure and set
.Va errno .
.Fn alarm
returns the seconds described above.
The raw kernel calls return a negative error number on failure.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The default suite's
.Pa tests/sigprobe.c
arms a single alarm with
.Dv SIGALRM
blocked and requires it not to end a sleep and its handler to run once
when the signal is unblocked, arms an interval timer and requires it to
end three sleeps, and requires a child of
.Xr fork 2
to have no timer.
The dhcp suite runs
.Xr arping 1
at an address nothing answers and requires its
.Fl w
deadline to end the run with status 1.
.Sh ERRORS
The kernel handlers return:
.Bl -tag -width "[EINVAL]"
.It Bq Er EFAULT
.Fa new
or
.Fa cur
is null or cannot be read or written, or
.Fa old
cannot be written, in which case the timer is already set.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
.Fa which
is not
.Dv ITIMER_REAL ,
or a time is negative or has microseconds of a second or more.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr nanosleep 2 ,
.Xr poll 2 ,
.Xr select 2 ,
.Xr sigaction 2
